Table 1: PSR J1537+1155 positions in reference to FIRST J153746.2+114215 caused, respectively, by Galactic gravitational potential (Damour & Taylor, 1991; Nice & Taylor, 1995) and by transverse motion (Shklovskii, 1970); \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm GW} represents the intrinsic orbital decay as a result of the GW emissions from the inspiraling DNS. The estimation of the two extrinsic orbital-decay terms rely on the distance to J1537, while \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Shk} also depends on the proper motion. On the other hand, the GR-based \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm GW} can be calculated, provided the orbital period P_{\\rm b}, the orbital eccentricity e and the masses of the two DNS constituents (Peters & Mathews, 1963; Weisberg & Huang, 2016), all of which have been precisely determined with pulsar timing (Fonseca et al., 2014). Hence, one can test GR by comparing the observed intrinsic orbital decay \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm int}=\\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm obs}-\\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal}- \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Shk} with \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm GW}. For J1537, this test is the one (among the tests with PK parameters, see Section 1.1) that showed the largest discrepancy with GR (see Figure 9 of Fonseca et al., 2014), possibly due to the unreliable DM-based distance used for the test.

Using Equation 22 of Weisberg & Huang (2016), we calculated \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm GW}=-192.45\\pm 0.06\\,{\\rm fs}\\ {\\rm s}^{-1}. Using the proper motion \\mu=\\sqrt{\\mu_{\\alpha}^{2}+\\mu_{\\delta}^{2}} and the distance D acquired with Bayesian inference (see Table 2), we updated \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Shk}=\\dot{\\mu}^{2}D/c\\cdot P_{\\rm b}=53\\pm 4\\,{\\rm fs}\\ {\\rm s}^{-1}. The uncertainties for \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm GW} and \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Shk} were derived with error propagation, which were subsequently confirmed by Monte-Carlo simulations. In the \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Shk} error estimation, we did not take into account the small correlation between \\mu_{\\alpha} and \\varpi (mentioned in Section 3). This is because the correlation between \\mu and \\varpi is still negligible, as the declination component dominates the proper motion (see Table 2).

Following Zhu et al. (2018), we estimated \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} with different Galactic mass distribution models compiled in GalPot ([https://github.com/PaulMcMillan-Astro/GalPot](https://github.com/PaulMcMillan-Astro/GalPot), McMillan, 2017). The \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} results are summarized in Table 3. The errors on \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} can be attributed to two sources: the uncertainty in the measurements (such as distance and proper motion) and the inaccuracy of the Galactic mass distribution model. The former \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} errors, at the \\leq 0.1\\,{\\rm fs}\\ {\\rm s}^{-1} level (see Table 3), were derived with Monte-Carlo simulations. We approached the latter \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} errors with the standard deviation of the \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} estimates listed in Table 3. For this calculation of the standard deviation, we do not include the \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} based on the analytical model by Nice & Taylor (1995), because **1)** the analytical model is oversimplified (see the discussion in Appendix A of Zhu et al. 2018) and **2)** the resultant \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} is inconsistent with other models (see Table 3). Accordingly, we adopted the average \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} of the four remaining Galactic mass distribution models (Dehnen & Binney, 1998; Binney & Tremaine, 2011; Piffl et al., 2014; McMillan, 2017) as the \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} estimate. In this way, we obtained \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal}=-1.9\\pm 0.2\\,{\\rm fs}\\ {\\rm s}^{-1}, where the error budget has included the standard deviation (0.14\\,{\\rm fs}\\ {\\rm s}^{-1}) of \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal}. As Galpot was not available in 2014, Fonseca et al. (2014) adopted the \\dot{P}_{\\rm b}^{\\rm Gal} based on the Galactic mass distribution model of Nice & Taylor

